it's great to be joined today by dr.  Alok Kanodia who's a psychiatrist  practicing in Boston Massachusetts he's  on the faculty at Harvard Medical School  and he's been using meditation along  with the latest advances in neuroscience  and psychotherapy to help gamers who may  be experiencing a video game addiction  get control of their lives thanks so  much for talking to us about this IV  first thing is there something unique  about video game addiction that is  different than other addictions  absolutely David thanks for having me on  the show by the way um so I think the  first thing to understand is that our  model of addiction is based on  biological addictions so things like  alcoholism for example is one of the  most common addiction and right now  there's like an opiate crisis across the  United States and so those substances  those are what we call substance use  disorders and so those are chemicals  that have a direct effect on a  particular part of your brain and so our  treatment of addictions tends to be  based on things like alcoholism but a  video game doesn't affect people in the  same way is like a molecule of alcohol  or a molecule of like heroin or cocaine  Gaming is much more complex and affects  people in a whole slew of neurological  psychological and even kind of social  ways can you talk a little bit about I  mean if we imagine two people someone  who may be playing video games 10 hours  a day and someone who may be watching  reality television 10 hours a day the  amount of time that goes into it is the  same the amount of time sitting in front  of a television is effectively the same  but these are two different behaviors  right absolutely can you talk about how  or why so if we think about someone who  watches reality television first of all  I I don't know I've never met someone  who watches 10 hours of reality TV for  like years and years and years whereas  I've met a ton of gamers who will be in  their mid-twenties and have really done  nothing except for play video games for  like six to ten hours a day for the last  decade  so I think just in terms of the  commonality of video game addiction is  way higher than let's say reality TV but  fundamentally if you think about the  person who's watching reality TV that's  entirely a passive consumption of  something  whereas video gaming is way more active  if you look at gamers part of the reason  that they stay so engaged with games is  because there are social aspects to it  for example like people will have you  know online friends on their Steam  friends list that give them a sense of  community that they actually get to know  very well over time I mean myself  included so I for example started  playing Starcraft with a guy I when I  was 15 years old had never met the dude  and then 12 years later I've been  playing with the guy like you know on a  weekly basis for the last decade I  invited him to my wedding and so I think  aiming has a very powerful community  aspect in which people form authentic  relations which keeps people kind of  plugged into the computer because as  human beings we want to connect with  other people and gaming actually allows  you to do that another big aspect that  separates gaming from kind of passive  consumption is that a lot of gamers  derive identity from the game so they  think of themselves as oh I'm a  competitive player in this game or  they'll create a character in a  massively multiplayer online RPG like  World of Warcraft or FF 14 or Elder  Scrolls online and they sort of identify  with with this person that they are in  the virtual world and they derive a  sense of kind of pride in their identity  I know a lot of gamers for example who  take a lot of pride in accomplishing  things within games and if you think  about someone who's watching reality TV  there's no sense of accomplishment there  ever and I think that's why video games  are such a big problem because they do  actually hijack parts of our brain that  involve reward circuitry that and will  involve overcoming challenges that  involve achieving things and so I think  the two are actually very different does  that make sense  it does yeah is it is there a way to  objectively say that video game  addiction or  equivalent addictions are quote less  serious or quote less damaging than  addictions to some of the substances you  mentioned or is it not so clear that we  can say that I think less damaging so I  think they're more damaging in some ways  and less damaging than other ways and  and you can sort of say that  scientifically for example there are  cases of people who died from video game  addiction but those tend to be very few  and far between so I can think of like  two or three case reports over the last  three or four years people who have died  a video game addiction whereas we know  that for example alcohol heroin cocaine  those will kill you like people died  from liver disease they die from  overdosing on alcohol they die from  overdosing on opiates and that's a very  common situation I think that so in that  way you can say that biological  addictions are more dangerous if we're  defining danger as you know risk of  mortality on the other hand I think  actually gaming addiction can be more  dangerous in a lot of ways because of  the way that it's insidious and sort of  takes control of someone's life there  are a lot of gamers who actually kind of  are very happy and and sort of fine with  playing games for like six to ten hours  a day and in that sense I mean there are  some alcoholics who don't think that  they have a problem as well but I think  because gaming is just so much fun and  because it engages people on so many  different levels like for example  earlier I was talking about how you can  form authentic relationships I think in  a sense it can be even more dangerous  because it's giving you something that  the real world gives you and therefore  sort of takes away the need for you to  engage in the real world and when I  think about how that shapes someone's  life I think it's very very dangerous  because you're sort of you enter this  virtual world and then before you  realize it you're not really able to  move in the real world and you kind of  don't know how you ended up there are  men versus women more or less likely to  be  addicted and I know that we need to  account for the fact that there are  gender differences in terms of who plays  video games right like there are way  more male gamers than female gamers to  begin with and there's also probably  different social stigma around mental  games versus women but what do we know  about whether men versus women are more  likely to become addicted so that's a  great question and and the answer is  unfortunately it depends so for example  one study out of South Korea that looked  at adolescents demonstrated that if you  for the people who were addicted there  was a four to one ratio of male men to  women in terms of who qualifies as a  video game addict but then there are  other studies that look at sort of that  aren't quite so specific and are a  little bit broad that suggests that the  kinds of video game addiction that each  gender has seems to be different so for  example women will are much more likely  to spend more time playing mobile games  or like iPad games or things like that  and you know men are much more likely to  play things like overwatch and so there  does seem to be a gender difference in  terms of how they engage but it's a  little bit unclear whether and there is  some data to suggest that video game  addiction is more of a problem in men  but then there are other pieces of data  that are less how can I say this that  are less high-quality which suggests  that the amounts of addiction may be  comparable if you look at it in the  right way  and one example of that for example if  you lump in Internet addictions into  social media addiction and gaming  addiction women are much more likely to  be addicted to social media and men are  much more likely to be addicted to video  games and so some people hypothesize  that that that's actually a common  disorder and it's kind of like an  Internet addiction in which case it's  kind of the same is that too complicated  of an answer no not for me and we have  an extraordinarily smart audience so I  can't imagine it would be to too much  for them either let me ask you this when  it comes to treatment of video game  addiction is it mostly psychotherapy  of behavioral therapy or I guess let me  ask it in a different way some  conditions like OCD or others that you  know we have different degrees of  understandings for we found that there  is some success in treating them with  different types of medication right  pharmacological treatment is there  anything like that for video game  addiction or is it mostly in the realm  of psychotherapy so that's a fantastic  question and the short answer is video  game addiction is so new that we  actually don't have a good treatment for  it  it hasn't been well studied in terms of  randomized control trials but once again  the most high quality research on video  game addiction comes out of South Korea  and there are some studies that show  that for example there's a specific  medication called wellbutrin or B  proprio n-- which has been shown to help  people treat it so it's a medication for  depression ADHD and can even help people  with nicotine addiction so smoking  cessation and that seems to be the the  medication that works the best or  helping people who have a video game  addiction problem at the same time there  are multiple studies that suggest that  cognitive behavioral therapy is  effective tends to be equally effective  to other kinds of therapy excuse me and  then there are some studies that suggest  that combining the two which tends to  usually be the case is superior to doing  either one in my personal experience I  think that video game addiction is is  kind of a complicated entity I don't  think it is a disease the same way that  let's say cancer is a disease and so  when I work with people with video game  addiction I try to understand what is it  that keeps them sitting in front of the  computer screen is it some form of  social anxiety is it because they're  afraid to take chances in the real world  is it because they just find the game to  be more engaging than the rest of life  which is frequently the case and I think  that ultimately the most effective a  treatment for an addiction is to really  target individuals and try to figure out  is this like a social thing is it an  identity thing is it like a dopamine  thing is it a way that you  hope with your problems and so you have  to kind of understand why the person is  addicted to the game and then you kind  of target that treatment what worked for  you I mean you people would have no  necessarily reason to know but you  almost failed out of college because of  excessive video gaming I don't know if  you'd call it a gaming addiction what  what ultimately worked for you I mean so  I found something else that really  interested me so I think the the big  problem with a lot of gamers and this is  going to be a little bit of a roundabout  answer but a while ago one of my  supervisors once explained to me that a  smart kid in school is actually like a  special-needs kid that if you have a kid  who's intelligent they actually require  a certain kind of stimulation and  challenge in order to keep them engaged  and successful at school and when I work  with a lot of video gamers there's a  very common pattern of these these guys  are smart they're actually smarter than  the average population and there's data  to support that and so early on what  happens with these kids is they go to  school and then they get bored because  you know you learn arithmetic on Monday  and then for the next two weeks all  you're doing is arithmetic because  school moves at the pace of the slowest  person in the class and then you give a  bright kid something like a video game  where the second you beat level one a  more challenging level is waiting for  you and that's incredibly addictive just  the intellectual engagement that moves  at your own pace is incredibly addictive  to someone who's smart and so what  happened for me is I found something  else that I found so engaging in that I  could kind of grow and move at my own  pace and learning and that happened to  be like meditation in Eastern medicine  and I basically decided that I wanted to  become a monk and found that just to be  really engrossing so when I work with  gamers now one of the the key principles  for treating an addiction and how to  overcome your addiction is to find  something that you care about just as  much to find a competing interest  absolutely yeah absolutely fascinating  and I'm sure I thought when you said  meditation I thought you were gonna say  downhill ski jumping you found which you  just loved so much more but  meditation makes more sense I think for  sure we've been speaking with dr. Alok  Kanodia who's a psychiatrist here in  Boston Massachusetts thanks so much for  talking to us about this thanks a lot  David

